# Installer-technology benchmark (benchmark/README.md): downloaded
# third-party release artifacts, derived canonical application payloads and
# built installers are all reproducible from the committed scripts and
# package definitions and are never committed themselves.
/benchmark/downloads/
/benchmark/canonical/
/benchmark/artifacts/
/benchmark/results/lab/jobs/
/benchmark/results/smoke/
/benchmark/results/*/smoke/
# A campaign's raw lab evidence — job documents carrying every installed
# file's SHA-256, session records, VM states, job folders and their logs.
# A passing row duplicates all of it from the canonical inventory and the
# campaign's compact lab-results.json, so it stays on the build machine
# instead of in Git (benchmark/README.md, *What a campaign commits*). The
# lab/ trees of the first two campaigns predate the split, are already
# tracked, and are historical evidence this rule does not reach.
/benchmark/results/*/lab/

# Compression-architecture spike (benchmark/compression-spike/README.md):
# downloaded artifacts, extracted payloads, benchmark-only tools, the
# spike tool's build output and its scratch files are regenerated, never
# committed; results/ and report.md are.
/benchmark/compression-spike/corpus/
/benchmark/compression-spike/work/
/benchmark/compression-spike/tool/target/
